WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:01.673
- [Narrator] For decades,

00:00:01.673 --> 00:00:03.810
Italian engineers have
been working on designs

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:05.790
for a massive project,

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:06.900
one that could help solve

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:09.100
one of Southern Italy's biggest problems

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:13.230
by connecting 5 million people
in Sicily to the mainland,

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:15.030
bringing jobs and growth

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:17.970
to the economically
depressed southern regions.

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:21.870
The final goal, a $12.7
billion mega project

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:23.760
called the Messina Strait Bridge,

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:25.200
which if completed,

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.110
will be the world's
longest suspension bridge.

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:30.750
- Nobody has ever built such a big bridge,

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:33.270
so it is an incredible challenge.

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:34.103
- [Narrator] Building a bridge

00:00:34.103 --> 00:00:35.700
that's more than two miles long

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:37.800
in an area with gale force winds

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:41.550
and frequent earthquakes
is neither easy nor cheap,

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:43.953
here's how Italy plans to pull it off.

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:47.670
To understand why this
trade is so dangerous,

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:49.620
here's a look at the tectonic fault lines

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:51.090
in the Mediterranean.

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:52.770
Experts say, this line,

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:55.470
which runs right under
the proposed bridge,

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:56.430
is where the Eurasian

00:00:56.430 --> 00:00:58.980
and African tectonic plates meet.

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:03.980
Notoriously active, it caused
a 7.5 magnitude quake in 1908

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:08.430
and one 50 years later,
which reached 6.1 magnitude.

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:11.670
The fault line also created
this narrow corridor.

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:13.860
When winds from the south funnel into it,

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:16.920
they pick up speed as they're
squeezed into less space,

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:20.400
at times reaching up
to 170 miles per hour.

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:23.430
Winds like this can be
devastating for bridges.

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:26.910
In 1940, the Tacoma Narrows
suspension bridge in Washington

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:29.250
famously collapsed in high winds.

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:30.083
At the time,

00:01:30.083 --> 00:01:33.240
it was the third longest
suspension bridge in the world.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:36.543
So how are engineers planning
to tackle these challenges?

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:41.250
We visited Columbia's
engineering lab to learn more.

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:42.420
This is Raimondo Betti,

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:44.070
a suspension bridge expert.

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:46.620
He consulted on the Messina
bridge's early designs

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:48.030
in the 90s.

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:50.370
He says that a suspension
bridge gets its stability

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:52.230
from cables that attach the deck

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:55.680
to sets of towers supported
with vertical struts.

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:58.800
- So when you have a car
going over this bridge,

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:00.390
the car is on the deck.

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:02.829
Then the deck transferred this load

00:02:02.829 --> 00:02:06.150
through the suspender to the main cable.

00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:10.920
The main cable distributes
the load to the towers

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:12.540
and to the anchorages.

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:14.130
- The Messina Bridge will be held up

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:16.260
by two gigantic towers,

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:19.410
each 60 feet taller than
the Empire State building.

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:21.480
The deck will be 2.2 miles long

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:24.240
and take about 10 minutes to cross by car,

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:27.690
making it over 50% longer than
the current record holder,

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:32.400
the 6,637 foot long
Canakkale Bridge in Turkey,

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:36.030
- Messina is over three
kilometers free span.

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:39.030
So now you are pushing the envelope,

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:42.240
not just a few meters,

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:43.740
but you are pushing the envelope

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:46.800
of about almost a kilometer.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:47.820
- [Narrator] While that jump in length

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:49.440
is a construction challenge,

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:51.180
it's also a solution to one

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:53.433
of those big environmental dangers.

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:57.000
Experts say that extra kilometer of deck

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:00.000
will help protect the bridge
from seismic activity.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.730
Imagine this piece of balsa
wood is a bridge deck.

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:05.163
- This cross section is this long.

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:09.690
If I take this piece instead

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:13.470
and I bend it, this one
is much more flexible

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:16.020
because this is much more longer.

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:18.000
- The idea is that in an earthquake,

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:19.830
the deck will bend slowly

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:21.840
registering less seismic activity

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:25.140
like a large ship in small waves.

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:26.820
Moving upward, one of the bridge's

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:28.620
newest engineering developments

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:31.080
is these hourglass shaped struts.

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:33.510
They let the legs flex
more than a traditional

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:35.913
cross shaped strut when there's a quake.

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:38.970
Between the flexible deck and legs,

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:40.860
the bridge could theoretically withstand

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:42.900
a 7.5 magnitude earthquake,

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:45.420
the highest of any structure in the world.

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:49.050
- Seismic is going to be
important, but seismic,

00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:51.210
we know how to design for seismic.

00:03:51.210 --> 00:03:53.910
The major concern in my opinion

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:56.160
for this bridge is the wind.

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:56.993
- [Narrator] On a bridge,

00:03:56.993 --> 00:03:59.340
strong winds can look like this.

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:02.730
- You might have excessive
motion of this bridge

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:05.250
sway left and right, left and right,

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:07.470
that impairs the traffic.

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:10.560
that people say, "I'm not going there."

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:11.640
- [Narrator] So to prevent that,

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:14.010
engineers created a new kind of deck,

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:15.450
the Messina type deck.

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:17.130
This cross section shows.

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:19.110
- Three box girders.

00:04:19.110 --> 00:04:22.620
The two others were for vehicular traffic.

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:25.410
The inner one was for train traffic,

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:28.710
and then this box girders are connected

00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:30.963
by a very tight skin of crossbars.

00:04:33.730 --> 00:04:35.070
- [Narrator] All these lanes and tracks

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:38.640
will be capable of supporting
up to 6,000 cars per hour

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:40.530
and 200 trains per day.

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:43.020
And see how the side girders are tapered.

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:46.020
- The crosswind will kind of move over,

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:47.310
like an airplane wing,

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:51.210
and will not create too
much excessive motion.

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:52.320
- [Narrator] Principles of the deck,

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:54.720
which have been developed
over the course of decades,

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:57.210
have been tested in recent years.

00:04:57.210 --> 00:05:00.330
- The Canakkale Bridge
use similar principle,

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:02.580
but it does not have the center one,

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:04.830
because it does not carry any train.

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:08.340
The Messina Bridge, this
is another challenge,

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:09.990
has been done for two,

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:12.630
but now you have to make
it three box together.

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:13.560
- [Narrator] With this deck,

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:16.320
engineers say the bridge could
stay standing with winds up

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:19.080
to 186 miles an hour.

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:21.030
That's as fast as the top wind speeds

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:23.520
of a category five hurricane.

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:26.850
But the high cost isn't the
only challenge to completion.

00:05:26.850 --> 00:05:28.380
Critics have highlighted research

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:30.510
that high numbers of migrating birds

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:32.550
could collide with the bridge.

00:05:32.550 --> 00:05:34.500
Experts say that could be devastating

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:36.240
to endangered species,

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:38.070
and there's even concern that the mafia

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:40.020
could influence construction contracts,

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:42.423
inflating costs, and adding risk.

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:47.130
Currently, officials plan
to break ground in mid 2024

00:05:47.130 --> 00:05:50.040
and finish the bridge in the early 2030s.

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:53.070
So for now, this new world's
longest suspension bridge

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:54.683
is still a decade away.

00:05:54.683 --> 00:05:57.350
(gentle music)

